{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## REPAIRED — store_integrity 13 → 0 (operator decision: delete)\n\n### Repair method — rebar library + the sanctioned tracker-commit door (no ad-hoc raw git)\n\nNo CLI/library/MCP verb can express this repair, so I confirmed the door before writing\nanything rather than reaching for git:\n\n- `rebar bridge fsck` is **audit-only by design** — its docstrings state the L9 audit\n  boundary (`bridge_fsck.py:199,342`, \"never writes\"), and its argparse exposes only\n  `--tickets-tracker` / `--output`. There is no repair companion.\n- `rebar fsck --repair` is scoped to the **event store** (retire folded sources, rebuild\n  snapshots); `fsck_repair.py` never touches `bindings`/`reverse`.\n- `rebar tracker-maintenance --clean` repairs only stranded index entries and foreign\n  top-level paths — not binding-store content.\n- `BindingStore.unbind()` **cannot** express it: it pops `reverse` only when the forward\n  entry still carries a `jira_key` (`binding_store.py:426-430`), and the missing forward\n  entry is precisely the fault. Every other `reverse` mutation (`:422`, `:564-565`,\n  `_retire` `:593`) is likewise gated on a forward entry existing.\n\nSo the repair ran through rebar's own objects: `BindingStore` → delete the 13 orphaned\n`reverse` keys → `BindingStore.save()` (tempfile + `os.replace`, atomic) → deliver with\n`rebar._store.push.commit_and_push_tickets_branch(strict=True)`, the helper\n`docs/concurrency.md` §\"Mutating the tracker\" names for committing pending tracker content\nunder the store write lock. **Zero ad-hoc git in the tracker; no code changes.**\n\nFour guards ran before any mutation, each aborting the write on failure: (1) the recomputed\norphan set must equal exactly {REB-410…REB-422}; (2) none may be retired (a retired key would\nmean tombstone was the right call, not delete); (3) none may have a forward binding under any\nspelling; (4) post-deletion, the forward map must be unchanged and no orphan may remain.\n\n### Before → after\n\n| measure | before | after |\n|---|---|---|\n| `store_integrity` | **13** | **0** |\n| alerting `binding_drift` cells | 0 | 0 |\n| `unknown_event_types` | 0 | 0 |\n| `bindings` (forward) | 2111 | 2111 (unchanged) |\n| `reverse` | 2124 | 2111 |\n| reverse-orphan delta | 13 | 0 |\n\nFull after-state from `rebar bridge fsck --output json`:\n\n    unknown_event_types: 0\n    binding_drift: would_terminal=0, local_gone=0, retired_overlap=0, dangling=0,\n                   unbound_jira=0, absent_in_window_unprobed=950, indeterminate=5\n    store_integrity: 0   []\n\n(`absent_in_window_unprobed` and `indeterminate` are non-alerting by design —\n`_engine_support/bridge_fsck.py:306` — and are unrelated to this repair.)\n\n### No collateral damage\n\n- Committed diff is **one file, 13 pure deletions, zero additions**:\n  `.bridge_state/bindings.json | 13 -------------`.\n- `bindings-retired.json` byte-identical before and after\n  (sha256 `bd8c8219…97b5` both sides); `get_rotation.json` untouched.\n- Deletion verified present on **`origin/tickets`**, not just locally: remote\n  `bindings`=2111, `reverse`=2111, orphans=0, `REB-410` absent from `reverse`.\n\nDelivery note: the first push raced a concurrent remote advance of `origin/tickets`\n(`cannot lock ref`); rebar's own auto-reconcile retry merged and delivered it. Re-verified\nafterwards that the deletion survived the merge — this mattered because the tickets branch\nresolves `bindings.json` as `merge=ours` (`docs/concurrency.md:414`), so the merge outcome\nneeded explicit confirmation rather than assumption. Tracker is now clean with 0 unpushed.\n\nAll four acceptance criteria are met.\n\n### Follow-up worth filing separately (NOT done here)\n\nThe `unbind()` asymmetry above is the mechanism that leaked these keys and can leak more:\nany path that removes a forward entry without a `jira_key` orphans its reverse key\npermanently, and nothing in the supported surface can then clean it up. That is a code\nchange, deliberately out of scope for this operator-decided data repair."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786532310435138001,"uuid":"74c2ecbf-79ef-45aa-b0c2-d839598be154"}